        "en" to GemDetails(
            "Ametrine is a bicoloured quartz that combines, in a single crystal, the violet zones of amethyst and the yellow zones of citrine. This division comes from crystal growth: iron is incorporated differently depending on which faces are growing (positive and negative rhombohedra), and under particular temperature conditions some of the iron stays in a state that turns violet after natural irradiation, while the rest gives yellow. The Bolivian deposit formed in cavities in dolomitic limestones, where hydrothermal fluids deposited large quartz geodes.",
            "Trigonal system. Quartz prisms and points, with colour zones in sharply separated sectors. No cleavage; conchoidal fracture. Hardness 7; specific gravity 2.65; vitreous lustre; refractive index 1.544 to 1.553; birefringence 0.009 (uniaxial positive). Cutters often choose elongated shapes (baguettes, emerald cuts) that show the two colours side by side, or fancy cuts that blend them. Russian hydrothermal synthetic ametrine exists on the market; some ametrine is produced by partial heating of amethyst, which must be disclosed.",
